Set up Project B on this Mac so it builds and its tests pass. Work out the commands
yourself; README.md in this repo has the same steps for a person. Stop and report if a
step fails in a way these notes don't cover.

What it is: a clean-sheet browser. Go managers in core/, web shells in shell/, a C++
adapter in engine/ on Chromium's content layer, at the tag in CHROMIUM_VERSION, with no patches.

The machine
- Apple silicon Mac. The full Xcode app selected with xcode-select; the command line tools
  alone can't build Chromium.
- Go 1.26 or newer, Node 22 or newer, Python 3. Homebrew is fine.
- About 150 GB free: the Chromium source is about 60 GB, a build output about as much again.

Steps, in order, each with how to know it worked
1. Clone the repo anywhere; every script finds the repo from where it sits.
2. npm install in shell/. That also brings Playwright for the mock tests; then, in shell/,
   npx playwright install chromium for its browser.
3. tools/fetch-chromium.sh. Long download. Done when it prints RESULT=ok and
   chromium/src is checked out at the tag in CHROMIUM_VERSION.
4. tools/build.sh. Done when it prints RESULT=ok and chromium/src/out/pb/project_b exists.
   The first build is a full Chromium build: about an hour on 32 cores, several hours on a
   laptop. Run it in the background and wait for it once; don't poll in a tight loop.
5. ChromeDriver of the version in CHROMIUM_VERSION, for mac-arm64, from Chrome for Testing,
   unzipped so the binary is .tools/chromedriver/chromedriver-mac-arm64/chromedriver in the
   repo. .tools/ is git-ignored. Its major must match the Chromium tag's; take the exact
   version when Chrome for Testing has it.
6. Run, from the repo root, the two groups that need no screen. Each ends with one line
   per test, and every line should say "passed":
   tools/test.sh mock       # Go's tests, and the shell on the mock engine
   tools/test.sh app        # the real app, through its driver and through ChromeDriver
7. Only if a person is logged in at this Mac's screen (tools/on_screen.py says):
   install deskrig (github.com/debkosh/deskrig), then ask them to run deskrig setup
   permissions and allow it in System Settings (you can't do that part),
   then tools/test.sh screen. The app's window shows on their screen while it runs.
8. Report each group's result, with any FAIL lines in full.

Known traps
- The real-app tests start the app themselves; don't start it first. If an earlier run
  left project_b processes behind, stop only those you started.
- A group or a test that lacks what it needs prints why, says DID NOT RUN and exits 2. For the screen
  group that means nobody is logged in at the screen, it is locked, or deskrig isn't
  allowed. That is not a failure of the app; say so and go on.
- tools/test.sh vm and tools/test.sh android need a macOS VM and an Android emulator
  (docs/vm-testing.md, docs/android.md). Not part of this setup.
- A machine that already has that ChromeDriver elsewhere: link .tools/chromedriver
  to that folder, or set CHROMEDRIVER.
- Packaging and releases need the signing keys, which aren't on a fresh machine. They're
  read from $KEYKOSH_ROOT/project-b, else .keys/ (tools/keys.sh). Not part of this setup.
- Don't edit Chromium's source; the project builds it unpatched.
